                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_right:    `<strong>That's the diagnosis.</strong> Not four topics — one problem: competent operators who have never led change through other people. Now you can design backward from that behavioural shift instead of bidding a $2M content menu into a $1.2M budget.`,
                    coach_r1_fourtracks:    `<strong>You've built four content tracks because the RFP listed four topics.</strong> But the discovery call said these leaders “manage budgets and cannot lead change.” That's one problem, not four — and you're about to bid a $2M programme into a $1.2M budget and still miss the need.`,
                    coach_r1_technical:     `<strong>This isn't a knowledge gap.</strong> These are competent operators who run a $9B hospital system — they don't lack finance or strategy content, they've never led change <em>through other people</em>. Deeper technical teaching answers the wish-list, not the need.`,
                    coach_r1_morale:        `<strong>Motivation isn't the problem.</strong> These leaders aren't disengaged — they manage budgets capably. The gap is behavioural: leading change through others. Recognition won't move that needle.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp scoping.</strong> One diagnosed problem AND a segmented population — a common change-leadership core, tailored by clinical vs operational role. That's the foundation that lets you design backward and fit it inside the budget.`,
                    coach_r1_uniform:       `<strong>One undifferentiated cohort won't fit in the room.</strong> 180 people can't leave the wards at once, and a chief of surgery and a finance director don't need the same thing. What's common versus tailored across your four waves of 45?`,
                    coach_r1_toponly:       `<strong>The brief is 180 leaders, not 20.</strong> An elite track for the top 20 leaves the change un-led across the rest of the system — and it isn't what Aurora is buying. Develop the population the CEO actually named.`,
                    coach_r1_shallow:       `<strong>You skipped the diagnosis.</strong> Designing before you've separated the presenting request from the underlying need is exactly how custom programmes lose bids and under-deliver. Name the real challenge first.`,
                    coach_r2_ratio_right:   `<strong>That's the 70-20-10 weighting.</strong> Tilting the design toward live projects, simulation and coaching is how senior leaders actually change behaviour — faculty content sets it up, but the doing is where it lands.`,
                    coach_r2_ratio_mid:     `<strong>A balanced blend, but still content-heavy.</strong> 70-20-10 says these leaders grow mostly through doing and coaching. Shift more weight to the experiential half before you lock the architecture.`,
                    coach_r2_ratio_low:     `<strong>This is a lecture programme in disguise.</strong> Weighting the design to faculty content for people who change through application ignores 70-20-10 — they'll enjoy the teaching and behave exactly as before.`,
                    coach_r2_ratio_high:    `<strong>Almost no content anchor.</strong> Experience matters most, but with so little faculty framing the projects lack the concepts to build on. Keep a 10–30% teaching spine to give the doing structure.`,
                    coach_r2_backward:      `<strong>Designed backward from the outcome.</strong> Pre-work → residency → applied project → reflection, weighted to experience — that's an immersive corporate residency, not a course. The journey serves the behavioural shift.`,
                    coach_r2_menu:          `<strong>A content menu is not a design.</strong> Assembling the requested topics into modules is the wish-list trap again. Design the journey backward from the change you diagnosed, not forward from a topic list.`,
                    coach_r2_frontload:     `<strong>Front-loading teaching inverts 70-20-10.</strong> Concentrating faculty content and bolting on a project “if time allows” means the experiential learning — where behaviour changes — gets squeezed out first.`,
                    coach_r2_onewave:       `<strong>One wave of 180 empties the wards.</strong> They can't all leave the floor at once and a single cohort kills the peer network. Four waves of ~45 keep the hospital staffed and build cohort cohesion.`,
                    coach_r2_manymods:      `<strong>You're drifting toward over-stuffing.</strong> This many modules across nine months strains people who run hospital wards. Comprehensive looks good on paper; fewer, deeper modules actually land.`,
                    coach_r2_overstuff:     `<strong>Your design has too many modules for people who run wards.</strong> They can't leave the floor that often, and your 18 faculty-days are already spent twice over. What do you cut so the rest actually lands?`,
                    coach_r2_thin:          `<strong>Too few modules to carry a 9-month journey.</strong> A handful of touchpoints can't sustain pre-work, residency, project and reflection across four waves. Build enough structure to hold the change.`,
                    coach_r3_practice:      `<strong>Practice-weighted faculty fits 70-20-10.</strong> Professors of practice who've led real change are more credible to ward leaders than a marquee lecture — and they free budget for the simulation and coaching that change behaviour.`,
                    coach_r3_marquee:       `<strong>You've loaded the budget onto star professors lecturing.</strong> 70-20-10 says these leaders grow through doing and coaching, not listening. What behaviour changes when a famous professor talks at them for a day — and what did you starve to pay for it?`,
                    coach_r3_sim:           `<strong>The bespoke simulation is the experiential core.</strong> A change-leadership simulation tailored to Aurora lets these leaders practise leading change in a safe rehearsal of their real context — that's where 70-20-10's “70” lives.`,
                    coach_r3_nosim:         `<strong>You cut the simulation to fund more teaching.</strong> That trades the rehearsal of leading change — the thing that actually shifts behaviour — for more content they'll forget. The experiential core is not the place to economise.`,
                    coach_r3_nocoaching:    `<strong>No coaching pool starves the “20.”</strong> Coaching and feedback are how application becomes capability for senior leaders. Cut it and the residency is a one-off event, not a development journey.`,
                    coach_r3_thinops:       `<strong>You've under-funded the operations.</strong> Four waves of 45 across nine months need logistics, a design team and materials. Starve ops and the delivery collapses no matter how good the design looks.`,
                    coach_r3_overbudget:    `<strong>Your design doesn't fit the budget.</strong> You've committed more than $1.2M — Aurora pays a fixed price. Win like this and you lose money on delivery. Re-balance until the costed design fits inside $1,200k.`,
                    coach_r3_thinmargin:    `<strong>You've priced at cost to win.</strong> Below a 30% margin the engagement doesn't sustain the roster you need for the next 40 clients. A custom unit that can't fund its faculty has no business. Where's your margin?`,
                    coach_r4_l3l4:          `<strong>That's contracting on outcomes.</strong> Change-readiness pre/post, a live improvement project per leader, and manager-observed behaviour give the CEO L3/L4 evidence she can verify — the kind that renews a $4M relationship.`,
                    coach_r4_mixed:         `<strong>Satisfaction plus self-report is still soft.</strong> A confidence survey is better than a smile-sheet but it isn't behaviour or business results. The CEO runs a hospital — give her L3/L4 evidence she can actually verify.`,
                    coach_r4_smile:         `<strong>You promised transformation, then proposed a smile-sheet.</strong> A 4.6/5 happiness score won't renew a $4M relationship. She measures outcomes — what L3/L4 evidence will you actually show her in 12 months?`,
                    coach_r4_stretch:       `<strong>≥85 NPS is an over-promise.</strong> Bidding a number above your own benchmark to beat the rivals is exactly the over-claiming the CEO probes for. Commit to what you can defend, then exceed it.`,
                    coach_r4_vague:         `<strong>“High satisfaction” is not a metric.</strong> A target the client can't hold you to reads as evasive. Name a defensible NPS number and the L3/L4 evidence behind it.`,
                    coach_r4_diagnosis:     `<strong>That's a board-ready pitch.</strong> You led with the diagnosis, defended the design and budget fit, and named the Year-1 evidence that justifies the renewal — fit, sustainable margin and verifiable impact, not a sales story.`,
                    coach_r4_overpromise:   `<strong>You promised to “transform leadership” across all four topics.</strong> That's the wish-list back again, now as an un-keepable promise. The CEO hears over-claiming and discounts the whole bid.`,
                    coach_r4_prestige:      `<strong>Selling star names isn't selling fit.</strong> The brand and the famous faculty raise perceived value, but the CEO is buying a behavioural change in 180 leaders. Lead with the diagnosis, not the letterhead.`,

